\begin{abstract}
Post-grokking collapse can destroy accuracy while preserving decodable task information. We study these failures in modular-arithmetic transformers trained with Muon on hidden weights and AdamW on embeddings and the output head. At five captured transitions, the preceding head retains accuracy on the updated representations, while the new head causes the acute failure. Fresh linear decoders trained on the original training split recover $98.20$--$100\%$ at collapsed checkpoints. The mean-dependent part of the head update nearly reproduces each failure; the complete updates locally descend the accurate loss and overshoot at their applied scale. Correcting cross-entropy arithmetic prevents joint train/test collapse through step $30{,}000$ in five matched branches. An RMS-normalized model still fails through an embedding update under accurate arithmetic. We also show that full task-aligned Fourier filtering averages logits over inputs sharing the answer, allowing a memorizer to achieve perfect filtered accuracy. Together, the interventions localize individual failures, establish surviving information, and delimit the numerical and Fourier interpretations.
\end{abstract}

\textbf{The mean-dependent head update reproduces the acute failure.} Before the captured failures, full-grid feature-mean norms reach $10{,}374$--$12{,}203$. Let $h_1$ be the post-update residual, $W_{U,0}$ the preceding head, $\Delta W_U$ its actual displacement, and $\mu$ the training-set mean of $h_1$. Starting from the rescued logits $W_{U,0}h_1$, the head-induced change decomposes exactly as
\begin{equation}
\Delta z=\Delta W_U(h_1-\mu)+\Delta W_U\mu.
\label{eq:followup-mean}
\end{equation}
The same training-estimated $\mu$ is used for all test examples. Adding only $\Delta W_U\mu$ nearly reproduces each failure (Table~\ref{tab:mean-update}); its test accuracy differs from the full update by at most $0.33$ percentage points. This evaluation-only intervention establishes local sufficiency of the mean-dependent term. The centered term also reduces accuracy in some seeds, including a drop below $90\%$ in seed $3$; the mean term is therefore one sufficient route at that event. Feature growth and optimizer history remain coupled before the failing update.

